# Maintenance of the rules and skills

The rules in `.cursor/rules/` are an index. They tell an agent where to look and when.
The skills in `.cursor/skills/` give procedures for specific tasks.

## Content

1. Put a link to the SSOT document for each rule. Do not copy the content of the SSOT.
2. Keep the two rules with `alwaysApply: true` short. Put details in a rule with `globs` or with a `description`.
3. Start each `globs` pattern with `**/`. The workspace has many roots.
4. Use the disk path in a rule. Do not use the playbook name of a repository (see `00-workspace-index`).

## Update the rules when the workspace changes

| Change | Rule to update |
| ------ | -------------- |
| New, removed, or moved repository | `00-workspace-index.mdc` (tables "Directories" and "Playbook name -> disk path") |
| New type of task | `00-workspace-index.mdc` (table "Tasks") |
| New or replaced ADR | `adr-topics.mdc` |
| Moved or renamed playbook document | each rule with a link to the document |
| New structure document for a repository | a new rule with `globs` for the repository |
| New skill for navigation or for a type of task | `00-workspace-index.mdc` (table "Tasks") |

## Language

Write all rules and skills in ASD-STE100. The norms and the self-check procedure are in `.cursor/skills/ste-writing/SKILL.md`.
Before you commit, run `bash .cursor/skills/ste-writing/scripts/check-ste.sh` on each file that you changed.
